<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    An immersive application for headsets, drawn by cranpose-xr through
    OpenXR. One APK carries the entries of every Android OpenXR runtime. A
    runtime reads its own entries and ignores the others. The headset's library
    lists the app, and opening it starts it as an immersive app instead of a
    window. The Khronos OpenXR loader, which the plugin adds as a dependency for
    this service, brings its own permissions and the queries it asks the runtime
    broker with.

    Two kinds of entry differ between stores, and the Gradle property
    `cranposeXrStore` (or `cranpose { xrStore }`) picks the values:

        meta        The Meta Horizon Store. The default.
        android-xr  Google Play for Android XR.
        open        Pico, HTC, Steam Frame and sideloading: every feature stays
                    optional.

    `adb install` reads no `uses-feature`. Only the stores filter by it.

    Entries and their sources:

    Every OpenXR runtime
        `org.khronos.openxr.intent.category.IMMERSIVE_HMD` starts the activity
        as an immersive app.
        https://registry.khronos.org/OpenXR/specs/1.1/html/xrspec.html#android-runtime-category
        Quest, Pico OS 5.9 and later, Wave and Android XR use the Khronos loader.

    Meta Quest
        https://developers.meta.com/horizon/distribute/publish-mobile-manifest/
        `com.oculus.intent.category.VR`, `com.oculus.supportedDevices`, a
        required `android.hardware.vr.headtracking` and
        `android:excludeFromRecents` on the launching activity are what the
        store's checks read (VRC.Quest.Packaging).
        `com.oculus.vr.focusaware` is recommended by SDL's list of manifests:
        https://wiki.libsdl.org/SDL3/README-xr

    Pico 4 and Pico 4 Ultra
        `pvr.app.type` is in Pico's own OpenXR SDK guide:
        https://sdk.picovr.com/docs/OpenXRMobileSDKv2/en/chapter_four.html
        `pvr.sdk.version` and `pxr.sdk.version_code` come from Godot's Pico
        plugin, which pulled them in for the Pico Store and the Khronos loader
        of PICO OS 5.9:
        https://github.com/GodotVR/godot_openxr_vendors/pull/180
        https://forum.godotengine.org/t/openxr-apk-is-missing-version-code-fields-pico-store-submission/68865
        The Pico category `com.picovr.intent.category.VR` is from SDL's list.
        Pico's documentation names no tracking mode entry to set for an
        OpenXR app: `com.pvr.hmd.trackingmode` has no source and stays out.

    HTC Vive Focus 3 and XR Elite
        `com.htc.intent.category.VRAPP` is in SDL's list and in Godot's
        Khronos plugin. The `com.htc.vr.content.*` entries are the Wave
        capabilities that VIVEPORT filters apps by. HTC's page does not say
        whether an OpenXR app reads them:
        https://hub.vive.com/storage/docs/en-us/ConfigureAppCapabilities.html
        The `wave.feature.*` entries (hand tracking and others) stay out: the
        app uses the controllers, and a required unknown feature hides the app
        on stores that filter by features.

    Android XR (Samsung Galaxy XR)
        https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/openxr/get-started
        A full space app that draws through OpenXR starts as unmanaged. The
        runtime library is declared with `uses-native-library`, as optional so
        that the other headsets install the app. `android.software.xr.api.openxr`
        is the feature that Google Play reads for the Android XR release track.
        No permission is needed: the permissions of that page belong to hand,
        eye and face tracking and to scene understanding, which this app does
        not use.

    Steam Frame and Play For Dream
        No manifest entry of their own was found. Steam Frame runs an APK
        in its Android container and shows the controllers as Oculus Touch:
        https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/steamhardware/steamframe/engines/custom
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.vr.headtracking"
        android:required="${cranposeXrHeadtracking}"
        android:version="1" />
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.software.xr.api.openxr"
        android:required="${cranposeXrOpenxr}" />
    <uses-feature
        android:glEsVersion="0x00030002"
        android:required="true" />

    <application>
        <uses-native-library
            android:name="libopenxr.google.so"
            android:required="false" />
        <property
            android:name="android.window.PROPERTY_XR_ACTIVITY_START_MODE"
            android:value="XR_ACTIVITY_START_MODE_FULL_SPACE_UNMANAGED" />

        <!-- Horizon OS opens the app as an immersive app, not in a panel window -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.oculus.intent.category.VR"
            android:value="vr_only" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.oculus.supportedDevices"
            android:value="quest2|questpro|quest3|quest3s" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.oculus.vr.focusaware"
            android:value="true" />

        <meta-data
            android:name="pvr.app.type"
            android:value="vr" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="pvr.sdk.version"
            android:value="OpenXR" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="pxr.sdk.version_code"
            android:value="5800" />

        <meta-data
            android:name="com.htc.vr.content.NumDoFHmd"
            android:value="6DoF" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.htc.vr.content.NumDoFController"
            android:value="6DoF" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.htc.vr.content.NumController"
            android:value="1,2" />

        <activity
            android:name="dev.cranpose.android.CranposeActivity"
            android:excludeFromRecents="${cranposeXrExcludeFromRecents}"
            android:screenOrientation="landscape">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
                <category android:name="org.khronos.openxr.intent.category.IMMERSIVE_HMD" />
                <category android:name="com.oculus.intent.category.VR" />
                <category android:name="com.picovr.intent.category.VR" />
                <category android:name="com.htc.intent.category.VRAPP" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
